      "text": "To find the product of the greatest common divisor (GCD) and the least common multiple (LCM) of 18 and 42, we start by determining the prime factorizations of these numbers. The prime factorization of 18 is:\n\\[18 = 2 \\times 3^2.\\]\nThe prime factorization of 42 is:\n\\[42 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 7.\\]\n\nThe GCD is the product of the lowest power of each prime factor that appears in both factorizations of 18 and 42. The common prime factors of 18 and 42 are 2 and 3. The lowest power of 2 that appears is \\(2^1 = 2\\), and the lowest power of 3 that appears is \\(3^2 = 9\\). Therefore, the GCD of 18 and 42 is:\n\\[18 = 2 \\times 3^2.\\]\nThe LCM is the product of the highest power of each prime factor that appears in either factorization of 18 and 42. The prime factors are 2, 3, and 7. The highest power of 2 that appears is \\(2^1 = 2\\), the highest power of 3 that appears is \\(3^2 = 9\\), and the highest power of 7 that appears is \\(7^1 = 7\\). Therefore, the LCM of 18 and 42 is:\n\\[42 = 2 \\times 3^2 \\times 7.\\]\n\nNow, we calculate the product of the GCD and the LCM:\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(18, 42) = 2 \\times 9 = 18,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(18, 42) = 2 \\times 3^2 \\times 7 = 126.\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Product} = 18 \\times 126 = 2268.\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple of 18 and 42 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2268}.\n\\]",
