      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the product of the greatest common divisor (GCD) and the least common multiple (LCM) of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\), we need to determine both values first.\n\n1. **Finding the GCD of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\):**\n\n   - The prime factorization of \\(18\\) is:\n   \\[\n   18 = 2 \\times 3^2\n   \\]\n\n   - The prime factorization of \\(42\\) is:\n   \\[\n   42 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 7\n   \\]\n\n   - To find the GCD, identify the common prime factors:\n   - Both 18 and 42 have the prime factor \\(2\\) and the prime factor \\(3\\).\n   - The lowest power of \\(3\\) in both factorizations is \\(3^1\\).\n\n   Thus, the GCD is:\n   \\[\n   2 \\times 3^1 = 2 \\times 3 = 6\n   \\]\n\n2. **Finding the LCM of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\):**\n\n   - The prime factorization of \\(18\\) is:\n   \\[\n   18 = 2 \\times 3^2\n   \\]\n\n   - The prime factorization of \\(42\\) is:\n   \\[\n   42 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 7\n   \\]\n\n   - To find the LCM, take the highest power of all prime factors present in both numbers:\n   - For \\(2\\), the highest power is \\(2^1\\) (since \\(2\\) appears in both factorizations).\n   - For \\(3\\), the highest power is \\(3^2\\) (since \\(3^2\\) appears in \\(18\\)).\n   - For \\(7\\), the highest power is \\(7^1\\) (since \\(7\\) appears in \\(42\\)).\n\n   Thus, the LCM is:\n   \\[\n   2^1 \\times 3^2 \\times 7^1 = 2 \\times 9 \\times 7\n   \\]\n\n   - Calculate the product:\n   \\[\n   2 \\times 9 = 18\n   \\]\n   - Then:\n   \\[\n   18 \\times 7 = 126\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore, the LCM of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\) is \\(126\\).\n\n3. **Finding the product of the GCD and the LCM:**\n\n   - We have determined:\n   \\[\n   \\text{GCD} = 6\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{LCM} = 126\n   \\]\n\n   - The product of the GCD and the LCM is:\n   \\[\n   6 \\times 126 = 756\n   \\]\n\nHence, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{756}\n\\]",
