      "text": "To solve for the product of the greatest common divisor (GCD) and the least common multiple (LCM) of 18 and 42, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. Find the GCD of 18 and 42.\n2. Find the LCM of 18 and 42.\n3. Multiply the GCD and the LCM to get the final answer.\n\nLet's start by finding the GCD of 18 and 42. The prime factorizations of the numbers are:\n- $18 = 2 \\cdot 3^2$\n- $42 = 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 7$\n\nThe GCD is the product of the lowest power of each prime factor present in both numbers. So, the GCD of 18 and 42 is:\n\\[ \\text{GCD}(18, 42) = 2 \\cdot 3 = 6 \\]\n\nNext, let's find the LCM of 18 and 42. The LCM is the product of the highest power of each prime factor present in the numbers. So, the LCM of 18 and 42 is:\n\\[ \\text{LCM}(18, 42) = 2^1 \\cdot 3^2 \\cdot 7^1 = 126 \\]\n\nNow, we multiply the GCD and the LCM to get the final answer:\n\\[ \\text{GCD}(18, 42) \\times \\text{LCM}(18, 42) = 6 \\times 126 = 756 \\]\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{756}$. \n\nEuler would have quickly taken this forward to compute the exact value of the product. Let's verify our steps with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Function to find GCD\ndef gcd(a, b):\n    while b:\n        a, b = b, a % b\n    return a\n\n# Function to find LCM using GCD\ndef lcm(a, b):\n    return abs(a*b) // gcd(a, b)\n\n# Calculate GCD and LCM of 18 and 42\ngcd_18_42 = gcd(18, 42)\nlcm_18_42 = lcm(18, 42)\n\n# Calculate the product of GCD and LCM\nproduct = gcd_18_42 * lcm_18_42\nprint(product)\n```",
